well i often tell the story that uh when  i  met a new acquaintance many years ago  and they asked about my family  i said um dylan's gonna have a great  life he's uh smart  he's athletic he's good looking and he  has lots of friends he'll be able to do  whatever he wants it was barely a month  ago  that dylan buckner took his own life  whatever stereotypes people might have  about kids that commit suicide  you know however it's portrayed in tv or  movies  was just not our experience with dylan  at all it was a very silent  very invisible killer that  didn't come to our attention until he  made his first suicide attempt in  september  and the family says school closures due  to the pandemic  played a part you know 450 000 people  and counting in the us have died of  covid  and that's a terrible thing but um i'm  fortunate  in that i don't know um any of any of  those people but i do know one person  who committed suicide  i learned after his first suicide  attempt while his depression  predates covid there really is no doubt  in my mind that the lockdowns the  isolation the lack of in-person  schooling the lack of  in-person interaction with his friends  the lack of football  absolutely contributed to his death and  dylan's family isn't alone  america the debate over whether it's  safe to reopen schools  now can't avoid what experts say is a  mental health crisis  a rising number of suicides in some  places  cries for help everywhere  fewer than half of students have  returned to this high school in suburban  chicago  there is a quiet an emptiness here in  these rooms there are virtual  counselling sessions  and the demand has increased for the  students that struggled  we started to see students that uh you  know  started to really show up with signs of  depression differently than they had  before  everything from not being able to take  care of themselves to  just not getting out of bed doing school  from their bed  and then not engaging in the class at  all whether you're five years old or 17  this developmental phase is happening  so we have seen this before when  students have trauma in their lives  and have a trauma history in their lives  there are times where that developmental  process can be slowed and impacted  as schools everywhere work to reduce the  covid risk  even a full return to class could come  too late for some  i would think if i could predict the  future that for the next five to ten  years  we're going to see students come through  that have picked up trauma history  around this  that are continued need special support  here in schools  hi i'm diana boone and this is my  routine i wake up over there and i come  over to this desk area  students everywhere can probably  identify with diana for almost a year  now her bedroom  doubling as her classroom it's been  pretty difficult for me  especially staying remote this whole  time i haven't had that interaction with  other people  as i'm used to but the people who are  struggling are really struggling  sometimes  and they don't feel like they have that  support from the school because they  just don't understand it  we spoke to her on her doorstep about  that feeling of isolation  it's been hard some days but then i just  kind of think about hopefully this will  be over soon hopefully maybe there will  be a better day tomorrow and i just kind  of get myself through it  but there are sadly so many stories that  end like trevor's  he was awesome and i know moms say that  about their kids  because every mom thinks their kid's  awesome but i'm not the only one that  thought he was awesome  but being away from school changed him i  saw  some of his light go out a little bit um  it made him sad  it made him angry you know he um  he kind of felt lost he even told me he  felt lost  trevor took his own life in october his  family saw no warning signs  his mother says the isolation too much  for him  i think that the kids they need other  kids  they need to be involved they need to be  you know  they need to be in a classroom with  their teachers you know  it's you're missing all that  interpersonal stuff  that's very important i think  there's going to be a lot of ptsd from  this you know there is going to be a and  as a country we're not equipped to  handle  you know mental problems or mental  challenges at all  so i think we're going to have a fight  on our hands  if you read about depression if you read  about the signs of suicide you know many  of the symptoms  are not not going to be easy to see and  it's really important to talk to your  kids  you know really talk to them and for  kids it's really important for kids to  trust their parents they love you  unconditionally  and you know tell them about you know  how you're how you're feeling  um but it's also important too that  friends talk to friends i mean the  reality of it is is dylan  as much as we loved him and i think he  loved us and i think we were good  parents and had a very  strong family relationship you know just  couldn't open up to us  uh about his struggles in the same way  he could to his friends  i don't want anyone to have to go  through this  it is the worst it is the absolute worst  i just miss him a lot we all miss him  it's heart-wrenching for all of us  the reasons for any suicide of course  are complex and there are no easy  answers  but there are families who believe their  loss is just one more  awful hidden cost of this pandemic greg  milam sky news chicago
 

